--- !u!114 &1583020676
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1583020675}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8d1c7df4379610b43a45d14d27d887fa, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::IntroController
  clickHint: {fileID: 712425590}
  fadeImage: {fileID: 398909636}
  storyPage: {fileID: 1325184328}
  pages:
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9ddd77f0290dd1f47a44ba92a1cee131, type: 3}
  pageDialogues:
  - 'The house was never peaceful. Every morning started with an argument, and every
    night ended with another. Sometimes Mom and Gran fought about money, sometimes
    family, neighbours, and sometimes about things I was too young to understand.

    Their
    voices filled every room of that old house until they became white noise.

    Until
    one day...They weren''t the only voices I could hear.

'
  - 'Gran knew before I ever told her, I don''t know how she knew, but she told me
    our family carried gifts that weren''t always blessings. Around my wrist, she
    tied a string of old beads and crystals, then started making these awful-smelling
    remedies for me to drink. They were bitter as hell, but somehow, they helped.
    For the first time, I didn''t feel afraid of what was happening to me, But Mom
    didn''t see it as a gift, she saw the same superstition she''d spent her whole
    life trying to escape. And she was terrified someone else would see it in me
    too.

    Mom didn''t want people talking about her daughter hearing voices,
    she didn''t want them treating me the way they treated Gran, but I don''t think
    she realised she started treating me the same way.

'
  - "The day she found my bracelet, she flipped, we packed our bags that afternoon
    and left before dawn. She said Gran was poisoning my mind with old beliefs, Gran
    said she was trying to protect me. I remember standing there between them, not
    really understanding why something that had finally made me feel better had turned
    into another reason for them to fight, neither of them ever spoke to each other
    again.\r\nYears passed but the voices never went away, Mom started taking me
    to doctors. At first, we went to their offices. Then the visits became private,
    she said it was easier that way. But we both knew the actual reason behind her
    choices. \r\n"
  - "After school, Mom was always waiting for me, I wasn't allowed to stay behind
    with my friends, no sleepovers, playdates, nobody was allowed to come over to
    ours\u2026I wasn\u2019t even allowed to go to prom, and whenever someone asked
    my mom why, she always had an answer ready...\r\n\"She's not feeling well.\"\r\n\"She
    needs to go to her tutor sessions.\"\r\n\"Maybe another time.\"\r\nEventually,
    my friends stopped asking, I watched them make plans without me and they always
    had jokes about things I was never a part of, the more Mom tried to keep me away
    from everyone else, the more alone I felt.\r\n"
  - "Nobody seemed to agree on what was wrong with me, but they all agreed that something
    was going on, every appointment was the same, they\u2019d stare at me with sorry
    eyes and asked me what I saw, heard, when it started and if there were events
    in my life that triggered these voices\u2026tablets made me feel numb, but nothing
    ever made the whispers disappear expect for the remedies that gran used to make\u2026After
    a while, I started wondering if maybe she was right, maybe I really was sick
    and there was something wrong with me."
  - "Eventually I gave in and I stopped asking if I could go places because I already
    knew what she'd say, I stopped trying to explain the voices and I just started
    to pretend that I was getting better. By the time I finished high school she
    finally decided she had enough, she said she couldn\u2019t do it anymore.\r\n\"I
    can't keep doing this,\" she said.\r\n\"You're on your own now.\"\r\nSo, I left.\r\n"
  - "With nowhere else to go, I found work at a small grocery store in a town secluded
    from the city, with not much experience I needed to make money somehow to pay
    for college, funny enough... It was only a few blocks away from the house I\u2019d
    spent my entire life resenting, I hadn't thought about that place that triggered
    the start of everything in years."
  - "The closer I got, the louder the whispers became, at first, I thought it was
    just my imagination, then I heard something call my name from a distance, it
    sounded like a child. I stopped right at the door holding my suitcase and for
    the first time in years...\r\nI finally felt hope, maybe the answers I'd spent
    my whole life searching for in hospitals had been waiting for me in this old
    house all along. \r\n"
  dialogueText: {fileID: 66449988}
  audioSource: {fileID: 1583020678}
  closetSound: {fileID: 0}
  swayAmount: 2.5
  swaySpeed: 0.4
